## 1. New imports

I added the shadow-variable imports:

```java
import ai.timefold.solver.core.api.domain.variable.ShadowVariable;
import ai.timefold.solver.core.api.domain.variable.PiggybackShadowVariable;
```

These are needed so `BlockDecision` can hold maintained values instead of recalculating from joins every time.

---

## 2. New shadow fields in `BlockDecision`

Inside `BlockDecision`, I added these 4 fields:

```java
public int assignedSeatCountShadow = 0;
public int assignedSkillSumShadow = 0;
public int assignedEmployeeCountShadow = 0;
public int producedHoursTotalShadow = 0;
```

### Meaning of each one

* `assignedSeatCountShadow`
  number of **non-unassigned, non-fixed** seats currently assigned to this block

* `assignedSkillSumShadow`
  sum of `skill(employee, block.opId)` for assigned non-fixed seats in this block

* `assignedEmployeeCountShadow`
  count of assigned non-fixed employees in this block
  this is used for average skill calculation

* `producedHoursTotalShadow`
  total produced hours for this block
  = `fixedAssignedHoursActual` + dynamic produced hours from assigned flexible seats

---

## 3. Shadow annotations on `BlockDecision`

I made `assignedSeatCountShadow` the main shadow target and connected it to both seat changes and block variable changes:

```java
@ShadowVariable.List({
    @ShadowVariable(variableListenerClass = BlockShadowsFromSeatListener.class, sourceEntityClass = CrewSeat.class, sourceVariableName = "employee"),
    @ShadowVariable(variableListenerClass = BlockShadowsFromBlockListener.class, sourceVariableName = "startDay"),
    @ShadowVariable(variableListenerClass = BlockShadowsFromBlockListener.class, sourceVariableName = "days"),
    @ShadowVariable(variableListenerClass = BlockShadowsFromBlockListener.class, sourceVariableName = "hours")
})
public int assignedSeatCountShadow = 0;
```

Then the other 3 are piggyback shadows:

```java
@PiggybackShadowVariable(shadowVariableName = "assignedSeatCountShadow")
public int assignedSkillSumShadow = 0;

@PiggybackShadowVariable(shadowVariableName = "assignedSeatCountShadow")
public int assignedEmployeeCountShadow = 0;

@PiggybackShadowVariable(shadowVariableName = "assignedSeatCountShadow")
public int producedHoursTotalShadow = 0;
```

### Why this setup

Because all 4 values are recalculated together from the same logic, so one listener trigger is enough.

---

## 4. New listeners

I added 2 listener classes.

### A. `BlockShadowsFromSeatListener`

This reacts when a `CrewSeat.employee` changes.

It recomputes the shadow values for that seat’s block.

### B. `BlockShadowsFromBlockListener`

This reacts when block variables change:

* `startDay`
* `days`
* `hours`

That matters because produced hours depend on those values.

So even if the seat assignments stay the same, changing block timing/hours updates `producedHoursTotalShadow`.

---

## 5. New recompute helpers

I added a small set of helper methods:

### `initializeAllBlockShadows(SinglePassPlan plan)`

Used to initialize all block shadow values before solving / score analysis.

It builds `seatsByBlock` once and fills the shadow values on every block.

### `recomputeBlockShadows(ScoreDirector<SinglePassPlan> scoreDirector, Integer blockId)`

This is what the listeners call after a relevant variable changes.

It finds the block, gathers seats belonging to that block, computes the 4 shadow values, and updates them through `beforeVariableChanged/afterVariableChanged`.

### `recomputeBlockShadowValues(BlockDecision block, List<CrewSeat> seats)`

Direct assignment version used for initial setup.

### `computeBlockShadowValues(BlockDecision block, List<CrewSeat> seats)`

This is the core calculation method.

It returns:

```java
new int[]{seatCount, skillSum, empCount, producedTotal}
```

